     "type": "intro",
     "title": "How Did a 167-Page Fable Outsell Almost Everything?",
     "content": "In 1988, a Brazilian ex-songwriter published a slim novella about a shepherd boy who dreams of treasure. Its first publisher dropped it after it sold roughly 900 copies. Today, **The Alchemist** has sold over **65 million copies**, holds the **Guinness World Record** for the most translated book by a living author (80+ languages), and sits on the shelves of presidents, pop stars, and skeptical literature professors alike.\n\nThat gap — between commercial failure and global phenomenon, between critical dismissal and devoted readership — is exactly what makes this book worth studying seriously. In this seminar, you'll read **Paulo Coelho's The Alchemist** the way a literature professor reads it: as narrative architecture, as allegory, as a modern revival of the fable, and as a cultural object whose reception tells us as much about readers as about the text itself.",
