     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Paulo Coelho: The Author Who Lived His Own Plot",
     "content": "**Paulo Coelho** was born in **Rio de Janeiro in 1947** to a middle-class Catholic family. His biography reads like a rehearsal for his fiction:\n\n• As a teenager he announced he wanted to be a **writer** — his parents responded by committing him to a **psychiatric institution three times**, where he underwent electroconvulsive therapy\n• In the 1970s he became a celebrated **lyricist** for Brazilian rock star **Raul Seixas**, writing over 60 songs\n• He was briefly **imprisoned and tortured** by Brazil's military dictatorship, which considered his countercultural lyrics subversive\n• In **1986**, at age 39, he walked the **Camino de Santiago**, the 500-mile medieval pilgrimage route across Spain — an experience he credits with returning him to Catholicism and to writing\n\nHis first book, **The Pilgrimage (1987)**, chronicled that walk. One year later he wrote **The Alchemist** — reportedly in only **two weeks**, because, he said, the story was 'already written in my soul.'\n\nFor a literature scholar, this matters: Coelho deliberately blurs the line between **author and parable**. His public persona — the man who abandoned safety to follow a dream — is itself a marketing of the novel's central thesis.",
