     "type": "concept",
     "title": "What Is a Fable? The Genre Coelho Revives",
     "content": "Coelho subtitled his book a **fable about following your dream** — a deliberate genre signal. The **fable** and its cousin the **allegorical novella** form one of literature's oldest traditions:\n\n• **Fable**: a short narrative, often with archetypal or flat characters, built to deliver a **moral lesson**. Think **Aesop** (6th c. BCE) or the **Panchatantra**\n• **Allegory**: a story whose surface narrative systematically points to a **second level of meaning** — characters and events stand for abstract ideas. Think Bunyan's *The Pilgrim's Progress* (1678)\n• **Conte philosophique**: the 'philosophical tale' perfected by **Voltaire in Candide (1759)** — a deceptively simple journey narrative used to test an idea\n\nThe genre's defining features, all present in *The Alchemist*:\n\n1. **Flat, representative characters** (the boy, the merchant, the alchemist — most have no names)\n2. **A journey structure** that doubles as a moral education\n3. **Repetition of key formulas** ('Personal Legend,' 'maktub,' 'the Soul of the World')\n4. **An extractable lesson** the text states openly rather than hiding\n\nCritically, this means judging *The Alchemist* by the standards of the **realist psychological novel** — round characters, ambiguity, subtext — is a category error. The fable plays a different game with different rules.",
