     "explanation": "The treasure-dream tale appears in Rumi's Masnavi and in the 1001 Nights — where the dreamer is beaten abroad and learns of the treasure at home from his very mocker, a detail Coelho preserves. The Hassidic version (Rabbi Eisik dreams of Prague's bridge; the guard's counter-dream points to Eisik's own stove) was popularized by Martin Buber, and Borges retold the Nights version in 1935. Knowing the genealogy is analytically productive: it isolates exactly what Coelho added (the Personal Legend doctrine, mentors, Fatima, alchemy) to a borrowed two-page skeleton.",
